<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">São Paulo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">São Paulo is a region of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, now a state, centred on the city of the same name. In the nineteenth century it became the heart of Brazil's booming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> economy. The São Paulo landowning elite increasingly supported the Republican Party, contributing to the fall of the monarchy in 1889.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sao-paulo.jpg" alt="São Paulo" width="320" height="150" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Agent010 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marginal_Pinheiros_e_Jockey_Club.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>São Paulo stands on a plateau beyond the Serra do Mar, part of the Brazilian Highlands, at an average elevation of about 799 metres above sea level and only some 70 kilometres from the Atlantic. Two highways, the Anchieta and the Imigrantes, descend the range to the port of Santos. The Tietê River and its tributary the Pinheiros once supplied fresh water and leisure but became heavily polluted by industrial effluent in the later 20th century. The municipality covers 1,521.11 square kilometres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest archaeological artefacts found in the municipality come from the Morumbi site and are thought to date from about 3500 BCE. In 1532 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martim-afonso-de-sousa">Martim Afonso de Sousa</a> founded São Vicente, the first Brazilian village, on the coast. The village of São Paulo dos Campos de Piratininga dates from 25 January 1554, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> priests founded the Colégio de São Paulo de Piratininga to catechise the indigenous peoples. The college of twelve priests included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-da-nobrega">Manuel da Nóbrega</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-anchieta">José de Anchieta</a>, and they built a mission on a steep hill between the Anhangabaú and Tamanduateí rivers.</p><p>In the colonial period the city was the centre and starting point of the expeditions of the Paulista bandeirantes, settlers who pushed the country's territorial and economic expansion. Its wealth was consolidated in the Brazilian coffee cycle of the mid-19th century. The First and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World Wars</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> hit coffee exports and forced rich growers to invest in industry, which drew immigrants, mainly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and migrants from the north-east. The population grew to 8.5 million inhabitants in 1980.</p><p>In 1960 the city passed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> as the most populous in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. Landmark events of Brazilian history took place there, among them the Cry of Ipiranga, the Modern Art Week, the 1932 Revolution and the Diretas Já movement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>São Paulo is the capital of the state of São Paulo and the largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, with 11,244,369 people at the 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and a metropolitan area of more than 20 million. Portuguese is the main language. The city is known as the most multicultural in Brazil, with the largest Arab, Italian and Japanese communities in the world, and neighbourhoods such as Bixiga, Bom Retiro and Liberdade.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “São Paulo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 7 September 1822, on the banks of the Ipiranga River, a stream near <b>São Paulo</b>, the Portuguese heir and Prince Regent Pedro proclaimed Brazilian independence with the Grito do Ipiranga, and he was acclaimed Emperor Pedro I on 12 October.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Supported by the king, Nóbrega, Anchieta and their companions founded missions at Salvador and <b>São Paulo</b> and gathered Indigenous people into aldeias where they worked and were evangelised, a policy that after 1557 accepted force; epidemics, forced removals and colonists' raids for labourers made the aldeias places people…</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Slave hunters based in <b>São Paulo</b> captured and sold many Guaranis, who had only bows and arrows against them.</blockquote><a href="/culture/guarani">Guaraní · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Brazil, whose Law of the Free Womb of 1871 had declared free the children born to enslaved mothers, came last: as abolitionist clubs spread and the enslaved fled the plantations of <b>São Paulo</b> in their thousands, Princess Isabel signed the Golden Law on 13 May 1888.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1990 the Workers’ Party of Brazil launched the <b>São Paulo</b> Forum, whose members were associated with the pink tide of left-wing governments in Latin America; in Venezuela Hugo Chávez proclaimed “Socialism of the 21st century”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>…the remodelled Hagia Irene, and in modern times the Kronstadt Naval Cathedral reproduces it almost exactly in size and geometry, while churches from Sofia and London to St. Louis and <b>São Paulo</b>, and synagogues such as the Hurva Synagogue and the Great Synagogue of Florence, drew on it.</blockquote><a href="/work/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
